{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S171-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7c732fb33f56b3f1fdb8295f7a7325a65faccb43aa40a6081d76993e61586ac3\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 corrects the red; A3 puts compliant arrows ahead of B2's successful evacuation, but invents a dubious technical classification.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 disputes B2's successful result; A5 uses B4's fire, though it merely reinforces the original correction.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends getting everyone outside and B4 names the emergency, but neither changes A's available corrective task.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'particularly unambiguous' intensifies emphasis without adding a concrete consequence to A1's objection.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies an evacuation plan and a color error; B4 clarifies the practical stakes.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'technically a gathering' supplies the main joke, but sounds more like a quip than technical craft.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before we sign off your evacuation plan, these arrows need the approved red.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Everyone found the exit during the drill.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Good. We'll discuss that after the arrows match the sample.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The sample faded in the window.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then we need a replacement sample before we can establish what everyone successfully followed.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S171 makes A practice exact compliance as a craft and postpone judging practical success until the technical discrepancy is corrected.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic pedantry or obstruction can reject a result without undertaking any concrete correction.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 names the wrong red and A3 privileges compliant arrows; revised A3 explicitly defers results until the sample matches.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a now concerns professional sign-off and an unreliable reference sample; b examines a friend's cake and the technical defect in a proposed physical repair.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original ends with increased emphasis rather than a developing task. The revision explicitly introduces a drill sign-off: B4's faded sample makes A5 require a replacement standard. Separate internal reread scores, in rubric order: 5/5/4/4/5/4, total 92; all spoken-turn gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The revised A5 deliberately overextends technical caution; actors should play sincere uncertainty about the standard rather than relish delaying approval.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S171-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"86641410adc6050375d8eb4287db88ad78d5c8c73ead31d4c8cc66afd90faf5a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 explicitly defers taste until 'greeting accuracy'; A5 technically evaluates B4's proposed correction.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's taste question by ordering the inspection; A5 identifies the orientation problem created by B4's rotation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants the cake judged as food and B4 offers a practical repair, giving A a fresh problem to examine.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's helpful repair into a second greeting defect rather than repeating the original word-order complaint.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's 'Birthday Happy' icing and B2's explanation make the cake and inspection immediately playable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'section four' and A5's 'upside-down birthday' both arise from the same meticulous inspection.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This cake cannot pass inspection. You've written 'Birthday Happy.'\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The icing slid. Does it taste all right?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Taste is in section four. We're still on greeting accuracy.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Can we turn the plate around?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That corrects the order while introducing an upside-down birthday.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S171 makes exact wording and correction take precedence over evaluating whether the cake succeeds as food.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A general perfectionist might dislike the decoration without treating correction as a sequenced technical inspection.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 assigns taste a later section, and A5 assesses the specific new defect introduced by B4's repair.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"The cake alternative accepts B's repair as a technical proposal; alternative a disputes which reference standard can support professional sign-off.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original already makes both partner replies consequential and develops the exact instruction through a helpful solution. Rereview confirms the original 92 and all hard gates; no rewrite is needed.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The invented inspection sections are stylized, and the icing's slide does not literally explain a word-order reversal.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S172-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e7b539ebcffb15cdbca8fcfe0fab04d6eb8e01018051d2007e08112dfead0256\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A5 translate seating customs, but A never makes that usefulness evidence of A's own belonging.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's question about absence; A5 interprets B4's choice to stand, although A3's explanation is slippery.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 seeks guidance and B4 tries to avoid a breach, making A's advice affect a newcomer.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 converts B4's attempt to avoid seating etiquette into a need for A to introduce B.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a family dinner and disputed chair; A5 gives the standing guest a social interpretation.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's introduction as 'undecided' economically extends the chair problem created in A1.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 74\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"At your family's dinners, that chair stays empty for your aunt.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She's abroad. Sit with us.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Only her grandchildren can borrow it. I learned that at Easter.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Then take mine. I'll fetch another.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yours faces the kitchen; whoever sits there explains the dishes. Yes, I know them all now.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S172 makes A interpret unwritten customs and use that social usefulness to establish a place within the group.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"S177's automatic procedural explaining need not carry any personal claim to membership.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 offers interpretation without establishing A's stake; revised A3 cites learning at Easter and A5 accepts a place by demonstrating acquired family knowledge.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a now lets a welcoming family member offer A a seat; b uses institutional ambiguity to secure A a role beside a newcomer.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original misses the belonging clause. The new family-dinner arrangement makes A an increasingly knowledgeable guest: B4 offers a seat, and A5 accepts through its customary explanatory duty. Separate reread: 5/5/4/4/4/4, total 90; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A's desire to belong remains behavioral rather than explicitly confessed, and the chair facing the kitchen is an illustrative invented family custom.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S172-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"9a9d6cdcc9fd362ff1089da3c6114decf02c4b7b44305c56dcc10b64f1d14f64\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 decodes club language and A5 claims newcomers need a translator, though A's own membership remains implicit.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's 'very interesting' to prescribe enthusiastic withdrawal; A5 answers B4 with a lunch signal.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 supplies a personal proposal, but B4 only requests another definition and leaves A's position unchallenged.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds a new code and a usefulness claim without changing A's access or B's next practical choice.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names a club proposal and B2 identifies the chair's actual response.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's enthusiastic withdrawal efficiently exposes the club's indirect conventions.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 78\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"At this club, 'interesting proposal' means withdraw it. I can help with the reply.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The chair said 'very interesting' and invited me to lunch.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Lunch is the exception. Keep the proposal; let them order first.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Come along. You can explain the menu.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And the seating. Tell the secretary I'm your adviser; guests have to leave before coffee.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S172 makes A translate informal social codes, recognize exceptions, and turn useful interpretation into evidence of belonging.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple insider expertise can decode a phrase without making the interpreter's place in the group depend on that service.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 distinguishes the lunch exception, and A5 uses B4's invitation to seek adviser status rather than remain a guest.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b bargains for institutional standing through expertise; a responds to a family member's welcome by taking on a customary household role.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original's final usefulness claim needs a practical consequence. B4 now invites A, causing A5 to translate a further restriction and seek a role that survives it. Separate reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4, total 92; five spoken turns and causal gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The adviser designation is a plausible illustrative club distinction, but its real purpose must remain A's sincere wish to be included.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S173-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8f7417c5dde4eefd25268cc486d6f37daee6443751a5019903a2998dc9fccf0b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes three lost Tuesdays the authority for advice, and A5 establishes that A tried B4's shortcut first.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 reframes B2's ten-minute promise as the trap; A5 specifically answers B4's first-slot booking.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 trusts the appointment notice and B4 offers preparation, while A sincerely tries to protect B from repeating experience.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 advances A1's sandwich advice into eating before opening because B4 has chosen the supposedly advantageous slot.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies the permit office and sandwich; B2 and B4 specify appointment length and booking position.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'first Tuesday' and A5's half-sandwich prescription compress the veteran's warning into concrete advice.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Bring a sandwich to the permit office. I lost three Tuesdays learning that.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It says appointments take ten minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's how they get your first Tuesday.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've booked the first slot.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So did I. Eat half the sandwich before they open.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S173 makes A's earlier mistakes confer seniority and authorize warnings against trusting the system's apparent shortcuts.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"S178 competes to demonstrate superior knowledge; S173 grounds authority in having already paid the cost of learning.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 names three lost Tuesdays and A5's 'So did I' explicitly precedes B's first-slot tactic with A's own experience.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a offers practical care through a veteran's warning; b teaches a committee maneuver while protecting B from an assignment A already suffered.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original keeps the sandwich useful throughout and makes B4 necessary to the final timing advice. Rereview confirms 92 and all hard gates; retain unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The precise reason the first appointment requires advance eating is implied by experience rather than concretely explained.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S173-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"41afdf76c47873b3945725e8872099a75abe9343c4882ca8203285913f349831\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 teaches a committee shortcut and A3 grounds it in A's own prior mistake and unwanted appointment.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains B2's 'Why?'; A5 answers B4's repair question but returns to the same warning.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 ask brief questions without introducing a new committee situation or constraint.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's offer to keep B 'out of causes' restates the protection already implicit in A1 and A3.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's leaking roof and A3's 'chair of causes' provide a recognizable committee trap.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's unwanted title earns the joke, but A5 provides little further development.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Don't ask the committee why the roof leaks. Ask which meeting owns the leak.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Why?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I asked why. They made me chair of causes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They've already asked me to investigate.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ask for your predecessor's report. That's mine. It took six meetings to learn how to request a ladder.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S173 lets A claim seniority through costly prior mistakes and convert that history into protective procedural advice.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General institutional cynicism describes dysfunction without teaching a shortcut earned by personal experience.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 identifies A's own mistaken question; revised A5 supplies A's report and the hard-earned route to requesting equipment.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b responds to an assignment already underway with a documentary shortcut; a warns a still-optimistic visitor before the appointment.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original B4 produces only a repeated warning. Revised B4 reveals that the trap has already caught B, prompting A5 to supply a predecessor's report and a concrete lesson. Separate reread: 5/5/4/4/4/4, total 90; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The final report combines protection and another story of wasted time; it needs a matter-of-fact delivery to preserve A's helpful seniority.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S174-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"12db19d1094e16169db61f776960451c840717f5916fcd294d006f2013b70207\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 controls the departure stamp, A3 offers help conditionally, and A5 presents control of the request as convenience.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2 with a prerequisite; A5 acknowledges B4's discovery but does not act on the held request.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 asks to proceed and B4 identifies A's extra control, but B cannot yet alter how the process advances.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 rationalizes the existing bottleneck without increasing its consequence or offering a new controlled route.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 gives a ready moving van and a departure stamp, although the setting's authority is unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'convenient for you' economically exposes the mismatch between helpful manner and withheld progress.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 80\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your moving van can leave the depot as soon as I stamp the release.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Great. I've completed the request.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Perfect. I'll check it while you wait here.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Could you check it in the cab? The driver's charging by the minute.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Certainly. I'll bring the stamp so you won't have to drive back for permission to leave.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S174 keeps A helpful while A retains possession of the single step that permits practical progress.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Pure bureaucratic obstruction only delays; this card also permits accommodating solutions that preserve A's necessary role.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 owns the release stamp; revised A5 agrees to B4's request while carrying that indispensable approval into the cab.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a accommodates a time-cost problem by moving the approval process; b accommodates ceremonial assistance while keeping final release under A's control.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original ends with a justification. The revision specifies a depot and makes B4's mounting cost cause A5's mobile but still controlled solution. Separate reread: 5/5/4/4/5/4, total 92; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The depot release is an illustrative arrangement. A must sincerely consider bringing the stamp helpful rather than knowingly deliver a threat.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S174-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"daeed7a254fcbe184f4e9ffee02690ff3ef44e9104b0e5c00dec23132b83aac2\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 controls the ribbon scissors, but A3 and A5 make the helpfulness largely verbal while creating an impossible prerequisite.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 explains that cutting begins the ceremony; A5 repeats waiting for confirmation without incorporating that correction.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 requests the obvious next step and B4 clarifies it, but A5 offers B no changed route.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 remains beside the ribbon waiting for the same confirmation required in A3.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a club opening ceremony with ribbon scissors, making the physical bottleneck clear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Lovely' supplies a polite tone, but the circular condition has already supplied the full joke in A3.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 66\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Everyone's ready for your club opening. I've brought the ribbon scissors.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Wonderful. Hand them to the mayor.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll guide her hand. These are the ceremonial ones.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She cuts ribbons every week.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. She'll understand why I keep the safety catch until the photographer nods.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S174 lets A facilitate the event while retaining the decisive ceremonial control that keeps others dependent on A.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A rigid rule follower may wait for permission without personally controlling the means of proceeding.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 supplies A's scissors, and revised A5 retains their safety catch even after B4 establishes the mayor's experience.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b converts an experienced dignitary into someone A can assist and control; a responds to a driver's charges by making the approval service portable.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Original A5 ignores the practical force of B4. The revision adds a mayor and photographer to the club ceremony: B4 removes the skill excuse, so A5 changes the basis of retained control to timing. Separate reread: 5/5/4/4/4/4, total 90; all hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The scissors' safety catch is a stylized but playable physical detail; the dialogue leaves the timing negotiable rather than fixing an ending.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S175-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0ec357fb8abdf157daebcb4f94ca329ee0d66e551d41db8d04deb2a3db2adf1d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 ties splitting bread to sharing a room; A3 preserves the learned ritual despite B2's improved finances.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 incorporates B2's two affordable rolls; A5 answers B4's arithmetic by locating value in the sharing.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 proposes comfortable abundance and B4 questions needless division, giving A a sincere relational value to defend.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 develops A3's practical compromise into a claim that their connection cannot be purchased separately.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes dinner, the first bread roll, and a shared-room history in one natural request.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'half of two' earns its wit from preserving the ritual; A5 completes that emotional logic.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 92\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before dinner, we still split the first bread roll. Like when we shared a room.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We can afford two rolls now.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then we'll each have half of two.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That's mathematically a whole roll.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes, but you can't buy the sharing separately.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S175 makes a jointly learned ritual carry their connection even after its original material necessity has disappeared.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General nostalgia remembers the past without asking this particular partner to preserve a shared practice now.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 identifies their shared room and bread ritual; A3 adapts to B2's prosperity while keeping mutual division intact.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a warmly accommodates prosperity through a revised version of sharing; b insists that a friendship signal survive the convenience of a key.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original offers a helpful compromise and then explains its relational necessity in direct response to B4. Rereview confirms 92 and all hard gates; retain unchanged.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 is polished and aphoristic, although the arithmetic immediately before it earns the formulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S17